1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,189
<font color="#000000">Tabular language models could unlock the
spreadsheets buried inside industrial companies,</font>

2
00:00:06,269 --> 00:00:07,940
<font color="#000000">but they need labeled data,</font>

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,829
<font color="#000000">and only scarce experts can annotate these
technical tables.</font>

4
00:00:11,909 --> 00:00:16,043
<font color="#000000">Presented at NeurIPS 2022 by Ringsquandl and Koleva
from Siemens,</font>

5
00:00:16,123 --> 00:00:22,006
<font color="#000000">this paper asks: can active learning cut that
labeling cost for sub-cell entity recognition?</font>

6
00:00:22,086 --> 00:00:26,544
<font color="#000000">Their headline finding is that a batch-diverse
gradient method, BADGE,</font>

7
00:00:26,624 --> 00:00:30,174
<font color="#000000">matches full-training performance with far fewer labels,</font>

8
00:00:30,254 --> 00:00:33,610
<font color="#000000">while forcing maximum table diversity actually hurts.</font>

9
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:35,923
<font color="#000000">Industry runs on spreadsheets.</font>

10
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:41,089
<font color="#000000">Operators track equipment, sensors, and vessels in
loosely structured tables,</font>

11
00:00:41,169 --> 00:00:48,335
<font color="#000000">and extracting that information automatically means
fine-tuning tabular language models on labeled examples.</font>

12
00:00:48,415 --> 00:00:53,635
<font color="#000000">But these tables use highly technical language only
a few experts can annotate,</font>

13
00:00:53,715 --> 00:00:55,782
<font color="#000000">so labeling gets expensive fast.</font>

14
00:00:55,862 --> 00:00:59,741
<font color="#000000">The paper frames this as sub-cell named entity
recognition,</font>

15
00:00:59,821 --> 00:01:02,424
<font color="#000000">a genuinely new active learning problem,</font>

16
00:01:02,504 --> 00:01:06,450
<font color="#000000">since each cell carries multiple token-level labels
at once.</font>

17
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:12,505
<font color="#FFFFFF">Active learning is built for this setting:
plentiful unlabeled data and expensive labels,</font>

18
00:01:12,585 --> 00:01:15,932
<font color="#FFFFFF">squeezing the most performance from every annotation.</font>

19
00:01:16,012 --> 00:01:19,877
<font color="#FFFFFF">But big transformer models can't learn one example
at a time;</font>

20
00:01:19,957 --> 00:01:22,592
<font color="#FFFFFF">they train in batches over several epochs,</font>

21
00:01:22,672 --> 00:01:26,601
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the acquisition function must choose a whole
batch at once.</font>

22
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:30,675
<font color="#FFFFFF">Pure uncertainty sampling grabs very similar,
correlated cells,</font>

23
00:01:30,755 --> 00:01:33,391
<font color="#FFFFFF">often from the same table, wasting budget.</font>

24
00:01:33,471 --> 00:01:37,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">Yet no one had studied active learning with tabular
language models,</font>

25
00:01:37,868 --> 00:01:39,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">the gap this work fills.</font>

26
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:45,622
<font color="#000000">The model is a tabular language model: a
transformer encoder that swaps vanilla attention</font>

27
00:01:45,702 --> 00:01:50,461
<font color="#000000">for a row-column visibility matrix with within-cell
positional encoding,</font>

28
00:01:50,541 --> 00:01:53,352
<font color="#000000">so every token is aware of the whole table.</font>

29
00:01:53,432 --> 00:01:58,863
<font color="#000000">A decoder tags each token over four entity types:
equipment tags, equipment names,</font>

30
00:01:58,943 --> 00:02:00,947
<font color="#000000">physical quantities, and units.</font>

31
00:02:01,027 --> 00:02:03,568
<font color="#000000">On top sits pool-based active learning,</font>

32
00:02:03,648 --> 00:02:08,206
<font color="#000000">repeatedly picking the most informative cells for
an expert to label.</font>

33
00:02:08,286 --> 00:02:14,054
<font color="#000000">Four acquisition functions compete: MNLP scores
normalized log-probability uncertainty;</font>

34
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:17,078
<font color="#000000">MNLP-plus forces round-robin table diversity;</font>

35
00:02:17,158 --> 00:02:22,523
<font color="#000000">BADGE clusters per-cell gradient embeddings with
k-means-plus-plus for uncertain,</font>

36
00:02:22,603 --> 00:02:25,480
<font color="#000000">diverse batches; and Rand samples uniformly.</font>

37
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:31,231
<font color="#000000">Evaluation uses a real industrial dataset, not an
academic benchmark.</font>

38
00:02:31,311 --> 00:02:37,235
<font color="#000000">Spreadsheets from several plants were downsampled
to at most five rows each,</font>

39
00:02:37,315 --> 00:02:43,081
<font color="#000000">and expert annotators labeled every cell with the
Prodigy span-based tool.</font>

40
00:02:43,161 --> 00:02:47,742
<font color="#000000">A random split gives 55 training tables and 24 for
testing.</font>

41
00:02:47,822 --> 00:02:53,272
<font color="#000000">The training set holds 4,774 cells but only about
1,100 entity labels,</font>

42
00:02:53,352 --> 00:02:59,118
<font color="#000000">just 0.23 per cell, meaning roughly 77 percent of
cells contain no entity,</font>

43
00:02:59,198 --> 00:03:02,910
<font color="#000000">an extreme imbalance typical of industrial data.</font>

44
00:03:03,290 --> 00:03:09,792
<font color="#000000">Performance is micro-averaged F1 on the held-out
test set across active learning iterations.</font>

45
00:03:09,872 --> 00:03:17,949
<font color="#000000">BADGE is the standout: it beats random selection
and even surpasses the ceiling from training on the full dataset,</font>

46
00:03:18,029 --> 00:03:19,594
<font color="#000000">using far fewer labels.</font>

47
00:03:19,674 --> 00:03:23,029
<font color="#000000">Pure uncertainty sampling with MNLP disappoints,</font>

48
00:03:23,109 --> 00:03:28,180
<font color="#000000">edging past random only after around 500 labels,
and with high variance.</font>

49
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:34,190
<font color="#000000">Most striking, MNLP-plus, which forces maximum
table diversity, is the worst of all.</font>

50
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:38,619
<font color="#000000">The four acquisition functions effectively ablate
diversity,</font>

51
00:03:38,699 --> 00:03:42,679
<font color="#000000">and the lesson is that the right amount matters
enormously.</font>

52
00:03:42,759 --> 00:03:45,362
<font color="#000000">BADGE's built-in batch diversity helps,</font>

53
00:03:45,442 --> 00:03:50,454
<font color="#000000">but MNLP-plus's forced maximum diversity hurts,
falling below even random.</font>

54
00:03:50,534 --> 00:03:57,335
<font color="#000000">Tracking how many distinct tables each method draws
from explains why: MNLP fixates on a few tables,</font>

55
00:03:57,415 --> 00:04:02,359
<font color="#000000">MNLP-plus spreads across as many as possible, and
BADGE lands in between.</font>

56
00:04:02,439 --> 00:04:08,070
<font color="#000000">The goal isn't maximum diversity but a careful
trade-off with per-cell uncertainty.</font>

57
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:15,336
<font color="#000000">The lasting message: active learning really can
slash the expert-labeling cost of tabular language models,</font>

58
00:04:15,416 --> 00:04:18,161
<font color="#000000">but only if diversity is handled with care.</font>

59
00:04:18,241 --> 00:04:22,433
<font color="#000000">Cell-level acquisition with built-in batch
diversity, like BADGE,</font>

60
00:04:22,513 --> 00:04:26,704
<font color="#000000">reaches full-training performance using a fraction
of the labels,</font>

61
00:04:26,784 --> 00:04:30,056
<font color="#000000">while bluntly maximizing table diversity backfires.</font>

62
00:04:30,136 --> 00:04:34,787
<font color="#000000">Balance diversity against uncertainty rather than
maximize either alone.</font>

63
00:04:34,867 --> 00:04:39,584
<font color="#000000">As the first work here, it flags two frontiers:
acquisition compute cost,</font>

64
00:04:39,664 --> 00:04:41,490
<font color="#000000">and annotator cognitive load.</font>
